{
  "id": "uami-vs-sami",
  "provider": "azure",
  "title": "User-assigned vs system-assigned managed identity",
  "summary": "A system-assigned identity lives and dies with one resource. A user-assigned identity is a standalone resource that several workloads can share.",
  "notes": [
    "SAMI: created and deleted with its resource. Simple and strictly 1:1, but role assignments must be recreated if the resource is rebuilt.",
    "UAMI: independent lifecycle. You can pre-provision it and its role assignments before the workload exists, which helps with IaC ordering and blue/green deployments.",
    "Sharing a UAMI is convenient but widens blast radius. Share only between workloads that need exactly the same access."
  ],
